1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,351
An agent reads a shared directory and a private salary
file, then creates a dashboard.

2
00:00:05,351 --> 00:00:08,518
Engineering can open the directory, but not the salary
file.

3
00:00:08,518 --> 00:00:11,052
Should Engineering be able to access the dashboard?

4
00:00:11,052 --> 00:00:13,903
TrustGraph answers by tracking the sources behind the
work.

5
00:00:15,103 --> 00:00:16,791
TrustGraph has three working parts.

6
00:00:16,791 --> 00:00:21,181
An agent input recorder saves the data and instructions
used for each run.

7
00:00:21,181 --> 00:00:24,557
A Rust policy checker decides who can access the output.

8
00:00:24,557 --> 00:00:27,934
A publishing gate checks approval before sending work to
GitHub.

9
00:00:27,934 --> 00:00:30,635
HydraDB stores the connected evidence behind those
decisions.

10
00:00:31,835 --> 00:00:37,173
Add TrustGraph as an MCP server in your coding agent, or
use its local HTTP API.

11
00:00:37,173 --> 00:00:41,844
The agent submits a task allowed by the operator and gets
a work ID.

12
00:00:41,844 --> 00:00:43,845
TrustGraph runs a fresh Codex turn.

13
00:00:43,845 --> 00:00:48,516
The agent checks progress, reads decisions, or requests a
new run with shared data.

14
00:00:48,516 --> 00:00:51,185
A human reviews and publishes the exact result.

15
00:00:52,385 --> 00:00:57,397
The YAML policy maps groups to source access, destinations
to their audiences, and reviewers to approval

16
00:00:57,397 --> 00:00:57,710
rules.

17
00:00:57,710 --> 00:01:01,782
Rust validates this structured policy and turns group
names into sets of people.

18
00:01:01,782 --> 00:01:06,481
The agent does not interpret the rules or decide whether
its own output is safe.

19
00:01:07,681 --> 00:01:12,248
Before generation, the input recorder saves source
versions, content fingerprints, and the task prompt.

20
00:01:12,248 --> 00:01:15,835
It links the run to every supplied input and its output.

21
00:01:15,835 --> 00:01:18,771
TrustGraph starts a fresh Codex turn with tools disabled,

22
00:01:18,771 --> 00:01:22,685
so the model uses the supplied data instead of fetching
extra information.

23
00:01:23,885 --> 00:01:27,286
Rust walks backward from the output through every recorded
input.

24
00:01:27,286 --> 00:01:31,708
It combines their access lists: only people present in
every list remain permitted.

25
00:01:31,708 --> 00:01:35,789
Here, the directory allows Engineering, but the salary
file allows only HR.

26
00:01:35,789 --> 00:01:38,510
So the generated dashboard keeps the HR boundary.

27
00:01:39,710 --> 00:01:43,334
The release check compares that permitted set with the
destination’s audience.

28
00:01:43,334 --> 00:01:48,605
Engineering is outside it, so TrustGraph returns the
restricting source and the path to the output.

29
00:01:48,605 --> 00:01:51,900
To repair the workflow, generate fresh from shared
planning data.

30
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:54,865
Rewording the restricted draft does not erase its
dependencies.

31
00:01:56,065 --> 00:01:58,507
Source access is only the first gate.

32
00:01:58,507 --> 00:02:01,996
Approval binds the output fingerprint, destination,
policy, and source-history context.

33
00:02:01,996 --> 00:02:04,090
Rust recomputes that binding at release.

34
00:02:04,090 --> 00:02:07,579
Change the file or context, and it no longer matches.

35
00:02:07,579 --> 00:02:11,068
The new version needs review; the earlier release stays
recorded.

36
00:02:12,268 --> 00:02:15,874
The simulator compares your current policy with proposed
changes, without publishing.

37
00:02:15,874 --> 00:02:18,169
For example, test a shorter approval lifetime.

38
00:02:18,169 --> 00:02:22,758
A twenty-minute-old approval is valid under a
thirty-minute rule, but expired under a fifteen-minute

39
00:02:22,758 --> 00:02:23,086
rule.

40
00:02:23,086 --> 00:02:27,347
Repeat with the same data, rules, and time to get the same
decision.

41
00:02:28,547 --> 00:02:33,366
Before publishing to GitHub, TrustGraph reads the evidence
from Hydra and checks its links and

42
00:02:33,366 --> 00:02:33,687
fingerprints.

43
00:02:33,687 --> 00:02:39,148
It checks access and approval again, then saves the
publication attempt in a durable log before sending.

44
00:02:39,148 --> 00:02:41,397
Repeated requests use the same recorded operation.

45
00:02:41,397 --> 00:02:45,251
If the outcome is uncertain, TrustGraph checks GitHub
instead of sending again.

46
00:02:46,451 --> 00:02:50,433
Workforce tools, pricing changes, incident fixes: the same
boundary applies.

47
00:02:50,433 --> 00:02:53,618
Inspect the sources, decision, and exact approved version.

48
00:02:53,618 --> 00:02:57,600
TrustGraph makes the path from coding agent to release
explainable.
